  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Reconnaissance / Gather Victim Identity Information — Email Addresses", "technique": "T1589.002", "what_happened": "At 2026-05-12 09:14:03 CEST, Suricata logged a sequence of HTTP POST requests from data.srcip=203.0.113.66 to data.url=/autodiscover/autodiscover.xml on the corporate Exchange server 10.108.57.184. Each POST carried an Autodiscover XML body containing a single candidate <EMailAddress> in the corp.local domain (administrator@corp.local, admin@corp.local, ceo@corp.local, cfo@corp.local, helpdesk@corp.local, john.smith@corp.local, alice.jones@corp.local, and more). The data.http.user_agent was spoofed to 'Microsoft Office/16.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Microsoft Outlook 16.0.13127; Pro)' to masquerade as a legitimate Outlook client performing first-run mailbox discovery. Crucially the requests arrived at a near-constant 12-second cadence — one probe every ~12s across ~17 candidate addresses — far too regular for a human and far too slow for accidental client retry loops. Autodiscover responds with measurably different response time and body size for a mailbox that EXISTS versus one that does NOT, so the attacker is using POST /autodiscover timing+size as a valid-mailbox oracle.", "chain_reason": "Suricata signature 'ET WEB_SERVER Exchange Autodiscover Information Enumeration' fired with data.srcip=203.0.113.66, data.http.method=POST, data.url containing '/autodiscover/autodiscover.xml', repeated against the same data.dstip=10.108.57.184. rule.mitre.id T1589.002 (Gather Victim Identity Information: Email Addresses) was attached by the rule. The consistent ~12s inter-request interval across many distinct candidate mailboxes is the automated-enumeration behavioural marker.", "first_seen": "2026-05-12T09:14:03+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Reconnaissance / Gather Victim Identity Information — Credentials", "technique": "T1589.001", "what_happened": "At 09:21:30 CEST — roughly a 60-90 second jittered pause after the Autodiscover phase ended at ~09:20 — the same data.srcip=203.0.113.66 switched to HTTP POST requests against data.url=/owa/auth.owa on the same Exchange host. The POST bodies were OWA forms-based-authentication payloads (destination, flags, forcedownlevel, username, password=x, isUtf8) iterating the first 10 candidate usernames (administrator, admin, ceo, cfo, helpdesk, john.smith, alice.jones, david.miller, laura.taylor, finance.team) with a deliberately throwaway password. /owa/auth.owa also exhibits a username-validity timing oracle: a valid username with a wrong password takes a measurably different time than a non-existent username. The cadence here was a near-constant ~10-second interval — again the automated-enumeration fingerprint. No password guessing is genuinely intended at this stage; the password 'x' is a placeholder — the attacker is harvesting which usernames are VALID via the auth.owa response-timing side channel.", "chain_reason": "Suricata signature 'ET WEB_SPECIFIC_APPS OWA auth.owa Username Enumeration Attempt' fired with data.srcip=203.0.113.66, data.http.method=POST, data.url containing '/owa/auth.owa', same data.dstip=10.108.57.184. rule.mitre.id T1589.001 (Gather Victim Identity Information: Credentials) attached. The jittered inter-phase pause (Autodiscover → 60-90s gap → auth.owa) plus the constant ~10s intra-phase cadence is the methodical-operator signature consistent with scripted identity reconnaissance, not a noisy bot.", "first_seen": "2026-05-12T09:21:30+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Reconnaissance / Gather Victim Identity Information — Cumulative Volume", "technique": "T1589.002", "what_happened": "Across the 09:14:03-09:24:51 window (~10m48s), Suricata aggregated ~27 distinct alerts with data.srcip=203.0.113.66 against data.dstip=10.108.57.184 — combining the Autodiscover Information Enumeration alerts (Phase 1) and the OWA auth.owa Username Enumeration alerts (Phase 2). The cumulative pattern — single external source, two consecutive Exchange identity-enumeration endpoints (/autodiscover then /owa), spoofed Outlook user-agent, and the consistent timing-interval cadence within each phase — is the canonical Gather Victim Identity Information fingerprint that MITRE T1589 describes. The timing-side-channel methodology (constant inter-request intervals to make response-time deltas measurable) is the behavioural marker distinguishing a deliberate identity-harvesting operation from background noise.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh correlation rule 'Exchange identity enumeration from single external source' (rule.groups containing 'suricata' + 'web' + 'recon') fired on aggregate count of Autodiscover + auth.owa enumeration signatures from the same data.srcip against the same Exchange data.dstip within a 15-minute window. The MITRE mapping T1589.002 / T1589.001 was attached automatically by the rule.", "first_seen": "2026-05-12T09:24:00+0200"}
  ],
